<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Yehud Medinata</h1><p class="st-meta">539 BCE – 332 BCE (approx.) · capital Jerusalem</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Yehud Medinata, the small Persian province of Judah centred on Jerusalem's rebuilt temple, where Second Temple Judaism took shape.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539 BCE, Judaean exiles were allowed to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. The province of Yehud was formed in the hills around the city, and under the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerubbabel">Zerubbabel</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> was completed c. 515 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Persian-appointed governors and the high priests of the temple shared authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nehemiah">Nehemiah</a>, a court official, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem's</a> walls c. 445 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezra">Ezra</a> brought the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> to the centre of community life. The province minted small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins inscribed Yehud and used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> for administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Yehud passed peacefully to Alexander of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> in 332 BCE and then to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. The texts and institutions shaped there formed the basis of later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yehud-medinata.jpg" alt="Yehud Medinata" width="320" height="514"><figcaption>Image: George Adam Smith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palestine_under_the_Persians_Smith_1915.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>539 BCE – 332 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jerusalem</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zerubbabel, Nehemiah, Bagohi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Hebrew</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yehud, Persian Judea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-436&amp;focus=yehud-medinata" aria-label="Yehud Medinata on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5588 1021 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yehud Medinata lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5908 1277L6140 1251M5908 1277L5548 987"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5548 987h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jerusalem. <a href="/atlas?year=-436&amp;focus=yehud-medinata">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

